The sensitivity study fixes one 999-array bank and 500 independent null observations per mask and compares pseudoinverse thresholds $10^{-8},10^{-11},10^{-14},10^{-16}$ and tie tolerances $0,10^{-14},10^{-12},10^{-10}$. The three scene masks have five positive covariance modes with condition numbers 1329--1505 at the default cutoff. All decisions agree across thresholds $10^{-8}$ through $10^{-14}$; at $10^{-16}$, one of 500 stripe-mask decisions changes. Reassociating the quadratic contraction as a matrix product and dot product leaves all ranks unchanged at cutoffs $10^{-8}$ through $10^{-14}$. At $10^{-16}$, the stripe ranks differ by up to 0.175 and one further rejection decision changes. Tie-tolerance changes produce no additional decision changes for the original contraction. Sparse masks with $L=2,3,5,17$ include deterministic and nearly degenerate profiles: thresholds near machine precision can retain numerical modes even when rejection decisions happen to agree. Thus the default cutoff is fixed and documented, rather than treated as mathematically immaterial. Full spectra, ranks and profile arrays are supplied. This instability near machine precision is a limitation of the implemented covariance standardization, even though the underlying exact moment formula is valid.

For a fixed, reproducible truncation, first symmetrize $\Gamma_s=(\Gamma+\Gamma^{\mathsf T})/2$ and let $s=\|\Gamma_s\|_2$. If $s=0$, set $Q_\tau=0$. Otherwise diagonalize $\Gamma_s/s=U\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_i)U^{\mathsf T}$. With $K=m+1$ and float64 machine epsilon $\epsilon=2^{-52}$, reject a computed covariance having $\lambda_{\min}<-64K\epsilon$; replace only negative eigenvalues within this numerical tolerance by zero. This check is an operational diagnostic, not a proved forward-error bound for the moment calculation. Fix $\tau=10^{-11}$ before testing, retain only $\lambda_i>\tau\lambda_{\max}$, and evaluate
\begin{equation}\label{eq:truncatedQ}
Q_\tau(p)=\sum_{i:\lambda_i>\tau\lambda_{\max}}\frac{[u_i^{\mathsf T}(p-\bar p)]^2}{s\lambda_i}.
\end{equation}
The $O(K^3)$ spectral factorization is separate from the $O(n+m^2)$ moment calculation. Save the scale, spectrum, retained rank, cutoff and tolerance together with the mask. Freeze this factorization before drawing or processing the tested observation and references, and use one batch evaluation rule for both. This yields a nonnegative fixed degree-zero statistic; its rank validity does not require the truncated matrix to be the exact inverse. The historical tables use the documented SVD contraction. A separate audit finds identical default ranks and decisions on all seven saved sensitivity masks; the retained ranks are $5,5,5,0,0,1,2$ for footprint, stripes, ellipses and $L=2,3,5,17$, respectively. Online Resource~2 retains the spectra and audit records.

A new power check uses 20 independent banks per scene mask, each with 999 references and 300 independently generated observations at $\rho=0.1$. Table~\ref{tab:bankPower} reports means and approximate 95\% Student intervals across bank-level rejection fractions; these intervals incorporate both bank variability and finite observation counts. They are distinct from conditional binomial intervals. Paired statistics within a bank do not constitute independent replications. Specifically, for $J=20$ and bank-level fractions $r_h=R^{-1}\sum_{a=1}^{R}\mathbf1\{q_{ha}\le\alpha\}$ with $R=300$, we report
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bankCI}
\bar r\ \pm\ t_{.975,J-1}\frac{s_r}{\sqrt J},\qquad
s_r^2=\frac1{J-1}\sum_{h=1}^J(r_h-\bar r)^2,
\end{equation}
intersected with $[0,1]$. The independent replication unit is one complete bank together with its fresh observations. If $\theta_h$ is the conditional rejection probability for that bank, then $\operatorname{Var}(r_h)=\operatorname{Var}(\theta_h)+\mathbb E[\theta_h(1-\theta_h)]/R$. Thus the interval estimates a model-specific mean rejection probability under this bank-generation design, not uncertainty over new satellite scenes; the Student approximation is not an exact binomial guarantee.
