@@BANK_ROWS@@
\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{table*}

The deliberately unmatched combined rejection in Table~\ref{tab:landsatCalibration} is decomposed in the raw component audit. In every mask, $V$ alone rejects all 450 observations at its Bonferroni allocation $0.05/3$. The union of the two profile-tail allocations rejects 95, 126 and 128 observations out of 450. Hence the combined value 1.000 is driven by a deliberately wrong support model. It should be interpreted solely as a preprocessing-failure diagnostic.

\subsection{Cosine and orthogonal-wavelet comparators}\label{sec:crossFamily}
We compare Haar scale energies with an orthonormal two-dimensional type-II discrete cosine transform (DCT) and periodized db2 wavelet scale energies implemented with PyWavelets \cite{Lee2019}. Every representation has the same band dimensions $(1,3,12,\ldots)$; DCT bands group index pairs by dyadic intervals of their maximum coordinate. Periodized db2 is decomposed to a single scaling coefficient, with all three orientations pooled at each level. Parseval is verified independently. The spaces differ despite equal dimensions: Haar has aligned block support, DCT has global cosine support, and periodized db2 uses different filters and periodic boundaries. Equal band ranks do not match localization, frequency response or boundary artifacts.

To compare representations under identical testing rules, all three use $H_u$, the footprint mask, the same Gaussian arrays within each bank, and matched references. This comparator does not substitute the Haar moment formula for DCT or db2 moments. Ten independent banks contain 999 references and 400 observations per energy condition. Table~\ref{tab:crossFamilyPower} gives the mean and standard deviation of bank-level rates. At $\rho=0.1$, the Haar--db2 difference is small; the results apply only to this frozen template, footprint mask, energy grid, boundary convention and statistic. They do not establish a general ranking of Haar, DCT and db2, or replace a task-specific imaging benchmark.
